course: arts
lesson: 36
title: AI pictures and AI music, as reported
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: AI picture and music apps make a dragon or a song from a few typed words, called a prompt, because they were trained on huge numbers of pictures and songs made by people.
explain: That raises big arguments, as widely reported: many artists say their work was used without asking, some people say typing a prompt is not the same as drawing, and others say AI is just a new tool, like the camera once was.
explain: AI can also make fake photos and fake songs of real people, so the smart habit is the same as with any shocking picture: check where it came from before you believe it or forward it.
explain: Learning to draw still matters, because drawing trains your eyes to really see shape and light, and nobody can type that into you.
explain: People say it their own way: "cooked" in Australia means ruined or wrong, "slaps" means sounds great, "sheesh" means wow, "laoshi" is Chinese for teacher, and "boomer" is a cheeky word for an older person who dislikes new things.
fact: AI image apps make pictures from prompts after being trained on huge numbers of existing images; whether typing a prompt makes you an artist is argued.
fact: It has been widely reported that many AI tools were trained on pictures from the internet without asking the artists, and some artists and companies have taken AI firms to court; the cases are argued and still being decided.
fact: Earlier AI pictures often had wrong numbers of fingers or messy writing; newer tools reportedly make fewer such mistakes, so checking the source is more reliable than counting fingers.
fact: In 2023 a song called "Heart on My Sleeve" spread online with AI voices, called voice clones, sounding like the singers Drake and The Weeknd, who did not make it; it was taken down from big streaming services.
fact: In 2022 a picture made with an AI tool won a digital art prize at the Colorado State Fair, and many artists said it was unfair; the debate is argued, and many contests now have rules about AI.
fact: Handing in AI work as if you made it yourself is dishonest, like copying a classmate; some teachers allow AI for parts of a project if you say so.
fact: Many AI apps have age limits, so children should check the rules and ask an adult first.
fact: Whether AI pictures are art is argued; when photography was new, some people also said it could not be real art.
fact: Making money from AI songs is far from easy money: streaming sites have strict, changing rules and have reported removing many AI tracks.
fact: AI can make realistic fake photos of real people, and checking trusted news before forwarding is the best defence.
fact: Many artists and animators still draw by hand even when they also use computers and AI, because drawing trains how you see.
exercise: Ethan made a dragon picture in five seconds with an app. What is fair to say? | He typed a prompt; calling that art is argued | He is now exactly as skilled as a painter who has trained for many years | The app drew nothing at all, Ethan really did every line of it himself | AI art is banned
exercise: What is a prompt? | Words you type to tell an AI what to make | A picture that is made carefully by hand with pencils | A person who whispers forgotten lines to actors in a play | A song by a band
exercise: Sarah's mum says AI firms trained on artists' work without asking. What has been widely reported? | Many tools learned from web pictures; some artists sued | AI invents every picture from nothing and never looks at art made by people | Every single artist was asked first, agreed, and was paid for their pictures | Every artist agreed and was paid
exercise: Max spots seven fingers in a picture. What is the best lesson? | Checking the source is surer | Every AI picture always has the wrong number of fingers on its hands | Seven fingers means that it must be a real photo of a real person | AI never errs now
exercise: Alina hears a new song "by" a rapper who never recorded it. What could it be? | A fake made with AI | A secret song that he recorded long ago and then forgot all about | A real song from one of his old albums that she had not heard | A cover of his song recorded by his best friend in a studio
exercise: What happened to "Heart on My Sleeve" in 2023? | Big streaming sites took it down | It won the biggest music prize of the year in America | It was officially released by Drake himself on his album | It topped the charts
exercise: An AI picture won a prize at the Colorado State Fair. What is true? | Many called it unfair; it is argued | Everyone, including all the other artists, agreed it was totally fair | It was disqualified at once and the prize given to someone else | AI cannot win prizes
exercise: Sun Yi makes her art homework with AI and says the teacher won't know. What is right? | She should say she used AI | It is completely fine as long as the teacher cannot tell the difference | Homework does not need to be honest, only tests and exams need to be | AI is always allowed
exercise: Ruby's nan says the knight dog picture is "not art". What is true? | It is argued | Nan is definitely wrong, because scientists have proven it is art | Ruby is definitely wrong, because it has been proven not to be art | Nobody anywhere has ever discussed whether AI pictures are art
exercise: When photography was new, what did some people say? | That it could not be real art | That it was the only real art | That painting should be banned | That cameras were magic
exercise: Lucas wants "easy money" from an AI song. What is true? | It is not easy money | Every AI song uploaded to a streaming site earns lots of money | Streaming sites do not have any rules at all about AI songs | AI songs are paid double, because they are cheaper to make
exercise: Fatima's cousin says "check before forwarding" about a silly photo of a leader. Why? | AI can fake real people | Leaders are not allowed to be photographed in most countries | All funny photos of famous people are always completely real | Forwarding is illegal
exercise: Ivan asks why he should learn to draw if AI can. What did his teacher say? | Drawing teaches you to see | Drawing is quicker than AI | AI cannot make any pictures | Only teachers need to draw
exercise: What should a child do before using an AI app? | Check its age rules and ask an adult | Give it their full name and school | Use it secretly at night | Nothing, all apps are made for kids
exercise: What do many contests now have about AI? | Rules on AI | A special big prize only for pictures made with AI | A complete ban on using computers for any entry | Nothing at all, because contests simply ignore AI
exercise: What is a voice clone? | AI copying a real person's voice | A singer's twin brother | An echo in a big hall | A recording of a choir
test: Your mate goes, "AI just invents pictures from nothing, it never saw any art." What is the best answer? | It learned from pictures by people | He is right, AI never looks at anything made by people | AI copies only one single artist each time it makes a picture | Humans secretly draw them
test: What would happen if you forwarded a fake AI photo of a leader to the family chat? | The fake would spread | Nothing at all, because fake photos stop spreading by themselves | Your phone would spot it was fake and delete it automatically | The leader would be told about it and would have to fix it
test: Your Gen Z cousin says a fake AI song "slaps, so who cares if he sang it" ("slaps" means sounds great). What is the honest point? | He did not make it; check | If it sounds that good, the singer must have made it himself | AI songs are always official releases from the record company | AI songs are official
test: In a cartoon, a girl wins the art prize with an AI picture and says nothing. What is the problem? | She hid how she made it | Nothing at all, because AI pictures are really drawn by hand | She should have used more colours to make it look handmade | Prizes like that can never be won by children anyway
test: Max says "that hand is cooked, deffo AI" ("cooked" means ruined). Why is counting fingers not enough? | Newer tools make fewer of those mistakes | AI never draws hands | Real photos always have seven fingers | Hands cannot be photographed
test: A Liverpool illustrator says AI firms training on her work "isn't sound" ("sound" means fair). Which is a fair summary? | Many artists agree; courts are deciding | Every artist in the world agrees that AI training is perfectly fair | There have never been any complaints from artists about AI | The law is fully settled
test: Ruby calls her nan "a boomer" for saying AI is not art. What is a fairer way to talk? | Give reasons; it is argued | Nan is old, so she is always wrong about anything new | Ruby is young, so she is always wrong about what art is | Nobody should ever talk about art at the dinner table
test: Sun Yi's friend says "laoshi will never know" (laoshi means teacher). Why does honesty still matter? | The homework is her own practice | Teachers always find out in the end because they have special software | Honesty only matters in maths and science, never in art lessons | Teachers use magic
test: Which is the best way to tell if a shocking picture of a real person is fake? | Find its source | See if it has lots of likes and shares from many people | Look only at the colours to see if they are too bright | Send a message to the person in the picture to ask them
test: A kid says, "AI can draw, so learning to draw is pointless." Which is true? | Drawing trains your eyes | He is right, nobody needs to learn to draw now that AI exists | Only robots need to learn drawing, so they can teach the AI | Drawing harms AI
test: Your friend says an AI song upload is "easy money, bro". What is wiser? | Read the rules first | Upload hundreds of AI songs and wait for the money to roll in | Use a famous singer's copied voice so that it earns more money | Pretend a band made it
exam: Identify the principal manner in which AI image generators acquire their capacity to produce pictures. | Training on many images made by people | By inventing images without any exposure whatsoever to prior work | By employing concealed human illustrators to fulfil every request | Copying one artist each time
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the question whether AI-generated images constitute art? | It is a matter argued in good faith by artists, critics and the public | It has been conclusively settled in favour of AI | It has been conclusively settled against AI | It has never been the subject of discussion
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the song "Heart on My Sleeve" of 2023. | Its AI voices led to its removal | It was recorded by Drake and The Weeknd together in a professional studio | It received the principal award of the music industry in that year | It is a nineteenth-century work
exam: Ascertain the conduct most appropriate where a pupil has employed AI in the preparation of homework. | The open disclosure of its use in accordance with the school's rules | The presentation of the work as entirely handmade | The concealment of its use from the teacher | The use of AI for all subsequent homework without mention
